 ### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Single Mode:** In the common case where a segment hits a single continuous surface, all four depth values are close to each other. The clustering results in a single mode with weight 1.0. This is the most frequent case [S1].
+*   **Single Mode:** In the common case where a segment hits a single continuous surface, all four depth values are close to each other, resulting in a single mode with weight 1.0 [S1].
 *   **Multiple Modes:** Multiple modes only occur for light rays that terminate on depth discontinuities as seen from the light [S1].
-*   **Precision:** The method relies on the ability to distinguish depth values. If the clustering threshold is too large, distinct occluders may be merged, causing overestimation of visibility. If too small, noise may be preserved [G].
